Dockerfile- It is file which is used to create Docker Images which is having an Instruction in the form of Key & Value Pairs

Dockerfile-1
-------------

FROM ubuntu
RUN apt update
RUN apt install tree -y
RUN echo "This is a DockerFile" >  /tmp/Dockerfile


docker build -t <image_name> . # Command to build a Docker image from a Dockerfile
                               # docker: Invokes the Docker CLI, build:Tells Docker to create (build) an image from a Dockerfil                                                                  # -t: Stands for tag.It assigns a name (and optionally a version) to the image.
                               # <image_name>: The name (tag) given to the Docker image.
			       # . : Docker looks for the Dockerfile in this directory where it is kept in this case it is root directory


Dockerfile-2
------------

FROM ubuntu
WORKDIR /tmp                                    # Sets the current working directory to /tmp/Dockerfile
RUN echo "Welcome to Devops" > tmp/sumeetfile   # Create a sumeetfile and write text into it
ENV myname Sumeet                               # creates an environment variable inside the Docker image/container, myname → Variable name,Sumeet → Variable value.
COPY linuxfile  /etc                            # Copy instruction copies the file or directories from your local  machine (build context) into the Docker Image. The linuxfile should be in the same location where Dockerfile is there
ADD devops.tar.gz /home                         # ADD instruction can do everything that COPY does plus Automatically extracts devops.tar archives & Can download  files from URL.
ADD https://example.com/file.txt /tmp           # ADD Instruction tells Docker to download a file from the specified URL during the image build and place it inside the image at the /tmp directory



Command for creating an Image from the above Dockerfile: docker build -t myimage .
Command for creating a Container from the above Image: docker run --name mycontainer -it myimage  /bin/bash


