Docker Volume:
---------------

Docker Volume is a directory inside the container. That directory we have to declare as a Volume
With Docker Volume we can share the data accross Containers

Advantages:
1. Volume Directory can be shared with the another container
2. They acts as a Hard Link(Synchronous Replication)

Hard Link - Let's assume we have two container i.e Container A and Container B. 
When Volume Directory of Container A is  shared with Container B. Then Volume Directory are on sync and visa-versa
Whatever update happen on Container A will same reflect on Container Bash


There are two types of Volume Sharing:
--------------------------------------
1. Conatiner to Conatiner Sharing
2. Host to Conatiner Sharing.


Practical Implementation:
---------------------------

Create a Dockerfile
vi Dockerfile
===========================
FROM ubuntu       # Uses the Ubuntu Docker image as the base image.
Volume ["data"]   # It tells Docker that the /data directory should be treated as a persistent storage location

docker build -t volumeimage .                                # Command to build a Docker image from the Dockerfile in the current directory.
docker run -it --name volumecontainer volumeimage /bin/bash  # Creates and starts a new Docker container named volumecontainer from the volumeimage and opens an interactive Bash shell. 

To verify: Put some data into the data volume directory
Exit from the container

Now, Share volumecontainer to other container i.e othervolumecontainer

docker run -it --name othervolumecontainer --volumes-from volumecontainer ubuntu /bin/bash  # Command to create a new Docker container that shares the volumes of an existing container (volumecontainer).


Now you are inside othervolumecontainer: create a file in Volume data directory. Exit from the Container
Verify other volumecontainer - You can see both the volume container directory are sync

docker start volumecontainer   # Command to start an existing stopped container named volumecontainer or <container_id>
docker attach volumecontainer  # Comamnd to attach your terminal to a running container named volumecontainer.

Go to cd /data
ls

Verify the content which is created in other volume directory container.
This proves that both the container volume directory are on sync

While creating the container declare the directory as volume

docker run -it --name container1 -v /dirA ubuntu /bin/bash  # Command to declare the directory as volume while creating the container1

To share the data of container1 with other container create container2

docker run -it --name container2 --volumes-from container1 ubuntu /bin/bash
verify by running the command ls you can see dirA

